= 1G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=1G}

An <Euler pseudoprime> is an odd <composite number> $N$ with $\gcd(b,N)=1$ satisfying $b^{(N-1)/2}\equiv(\frac bN)\pmod N$, where the right side is the <Jacobi symbol>. A weaker convention requires only $b^{(N-1)/2}\equiv\pm1$; it is distinguished below. A <strong pseudoprime>, with $N-1=2^s d$ and $d$ odd, satisfies $b^d\equiv1$ or $b^{2^j d}\equiv-1$ for some $0\leq j<s$.

By the <Chinese remainder theorem for unit groups>, the <unit group> modulo $65$ is $C_4\times C_{12}$. Modulo $5$, $b^{32}=1$. Thus the only possible sign in $b^{32}\equiv\pm1\pmod{65}$ is positive. Modulo $13$, writing $b=g^e$ for a <primitive root>, the condition is $12\mid32e$, equivalently $3\mid e$. Consequently $b$ has <multiplicative order> dividing $4$ in each factor. Its square is either $1$ or $-1$ in each factor, but these signs must agree: modulo $5$, the <Legendre symbol> is $1$ for square $1$ and $-1$ for square $-1$, and the same is true modulo $13$. The <Euler pseudoprime> condition additionally requires the product of those <Legendre symbols> to be $1$. Hence
$$
\boxed{65\text{ is an Euler pseudoprime to }b\iff b^2\equiv\pm1\pmod{65}.}
$$
Each sign has two roots modulo each <prime>, hence four roots modulo $65$. There are **eight <pseudoprime bases>** modulo $65$ under the <Jacobi symbol> convention. Under the weaker sign-only convention there are instead sixteen <pseudoprime bases>, since the two signs of the squares need not agree; the stated equivalence therefore specifically uses the <Jacobi symbol> convention.

For the <strong pseudoprime> condition, $s=6,d=1$. Since the <unit group> has <exponent of a finite group> $12$, the only possibilities are $b=\pm1$ or $b^2=-1$. The six <pseudoprime bases> are $1,8,18,47,57,64$. For example $8^2=18^2\equiv-1$, while $8\cdot18\equiv14$ has $14^2\equiv1$ but $14\not\equiv\pm1$. Thus **the strong-pseudoprime <pseudoprime bases> are not a <subgroup>**.

= 2F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Topics In Analysis}

= a
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $D$ be the closed unit <disc> and set
$$
 f(x)=x-\frac{g(Kx)}K.
$$
The displacement bound gives $\|f(x)\|\leq1$, so $f:D\to D$ is <continuous>. The <Brouwer fixed-point theorem> applies because $D$ is nonempty, <compact> and <convex>. At a <fixed point> $x_*$, $x_*=x_*-g(Kx_*)/K$. Therefore $\boxed{t=Kx_*,\quad g(t)=0}$.

= b
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For arbitrary $y\in\mathbb R^2$, the <continuous map> $g_y(x)=g(x)-y$ satisfies $\|g_y(x)-x\|\leq K+\|y\|$. Applying the preceding <Brouwer fixed-point theorem> argument with this positive bound gives $g_y(t)=0$. Hence $g(t)=y$, and **$g$ is surjective**.

= c
{parent=2f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $e=(1,0)$ and define
$$
 g(x)=\begin{cases}e,&x=0,\\x,&x\ne0.\end{cases}
$$
Then $\|g(x)-x\|\leq1$ everywhere, but $0$ is not in the <image of a function>. The map is <discontinuous> at $0$, giving **a bounded-displacement map which is not surjective**.

= 3G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Coding & Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=3G}

Identify a binary word with its coefficient <polynomial> in $R=\mathbb F_2[X]/(X^7-1)$. A <cyclic code> is exactly an <ideal> of $R$: closure under multiplication by $X$ is closure under cyclic shift. Its inverse image in the <principal ideal domain> $\mathbb F_2[X]$ has a unique monic <generator polynomial of a cyclic code> $g$ dividing $X^7-1$. Put
$$
 a=X+1,\quad b=X^3+X+1,\quad c=X^3+X^2+1;\qquad X^7-1=abc.
$$
The cubics have no root in $\mathbb F_2$, hence are <irreducible polynomials>. The eight monic <divisors> yield all <binary cyclic codes of length seven>. A code generated by $g$ has <basis> $g,Xg,\ldots,X^{6-\deg g}g$ and <dimension> $7-\deg g$, with the zero code treated separately.

For the <dual of a cyclic code>, let $h=(X^7-1)/g$. Orthogonality of coefficient <vectors> to all cyclic shifts gives the generator $h^*(X)=X^{\deg h}h(X^{-1})$. Here $a^*=a,b^*=c,c^*=b$. Thus the complete pairing, with the respective <dimensions>, is
$$
\boxed{1\ (7)\longleftrightarrow abc\ (0),\quad
 a\ (6)\longleftrightarrow bc\ (1),\quad
 b\ (4)\longleftrightarrow ab\ (3),\quad
 c\ (4)\longleftrightarrow ac\ (3).}
$$
These are respectively the whole-space/zero, even-weight/repetition, and two <Hamming code>/<binary simplex code> pairs. The reciprocal formula follows because the product of a word <polynomial> and the reversed <polynomial> of an orthogonal word has zero coefficients in all cyclic positions; the resulting inclusion has the correct complementary <dimension>, hence is equality.

= 4H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Automata and Formal Languages}

= a
{parent=4h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In strict <Chomsky normal form>, every production of a <context-free grammar> is $A\to BC$ or $A\to a$, with $A,B,C$ <nonterminal symbols> and $a$ a <terminal symbol>. Such a derivation cannot produce the <empty word>: it starts with one <nonterminal symbol>, and every step either increases the symbol count or replaces one symbol by one symbol. The <context-free language> $\{\varepsilon\}$, generated by $S\to\varepsilon$, is therefore **not generated by any strict CNF grammar**.

The convention matters: extended <Chomsky normal form> permits $S\to\varepsilon$ with $S$ excluded from right-hand sides. Under that convention every <context-free language> has such a grammar, so the requested example would not exist.

= b
{parent=4h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <context-free languages> $L_1=\{a^n b^n c^m:n,m\geq0\}$ and $L_2=\{a^m b^n c^n:n,m\geq0\}$ have <context-free grammars> $S\to TC$, $T\to aTb\mid\varepsilon$, $C\to cC\mid\varepsilon$, and $S\to AT$, $A\to aA\mid\varepsilon$, $T\to bTc\mid\varepsilon$, respectively.

Their <intersection> is $L=\{a^n b^n c^n:n\geq0\}$. Suppose $L$ satisfied the <pumping lemma for context-free languages> with length $p$. In a decomposition $a^p b^p c^p=uvwxy$ with $|vwx|\leq p$ and $|vx|>0$, the window $vwx$ cannot meet both the $a$ and $c$ blocks. Pumping down changes at least one count and leaves at least one count unchanged. Thus the three counts are unequal, or the word order is invalid, contrary to the <pumping lemma for context-free languages>. Hence **CFLs are not closed under <intersection>**.

= c
{parent=4h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

<Context-free languages> are closed under <union>: use a new start symbol with alternatives for two renamed <context-free grammars>. If they were closed under <complement> in $\Sigma^*$, <De Morgan's laws> would give
$$
 L_1\cap L_2=\Sigma^*\setminus\bigl((\Sigma^*\setminus L_1)\cup(\Sigma^*\setminus L_2)\bigr),
$$
contradicting the preceding <intersection> example. Therefore **some CFL has a non-context-free <complement>**. This is an existence argument; it does not assert that both <complements> of the particular $L_1,L_2$ are non-context-free.

A concrete choice is $L=\{a,b,c\}^*\setminus\{a^n b^n c^n:n\geq0\}$. Words outside $a^*b^*c^*$ form a <regular language>. Among words inside it, membership in $L$ means the first two counts or the last two counts differ. Each unequal-count comparison has a <context-free grammar>: for $a^i b^j$ with $i>j$, use $T\to aTb\mid A$, $A\to aA\mid a$; for $i<j$, replace $A$ by $B\to bB\mid b$. Append any number of $c$'s, or prefix any number of $a$'s for the analogous $b,c$ comparison. Taking these <unions> gives a <context-free language> whose <complement> is the non-context-free equal-three-count language. These productions are definitions of the example, not a restriction to strict <Chomsky normal form>.

= 5J
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= Solution
{parent=5J}

Write $\ell(\beta)$ for the <log-likelihood> and $U=\nabla\ell$ for the <score function>. <Newton method> uses the <observed Fisher information> $M=-\nabla^2\ell$; <Fisher scoring> uses the <Fisher information> $M=\mathbb E_\beta[-\nabla^2\ell]$.

For independent responses in an <exponential family>, with fixed <dispersion parameter> and fixed weights $a_i$, a <canonical link function> gives $\theta_i=x_i^T\beta$ and
$$
 \ell(\beta)=\sum_i\frac{y_i\theta_i-b(\theta_i)}{a_i}+\sum_i c_i(y_i),\qquad
 U=\sum_i\frac{x_i(y_i-b'(\theta_i))}{a_i},\qquad
 -\nabla^2\ell=\sum_i\frac{b''(\theta_i)}{a_i}x_ix_i^T.
$$
The last <matrix> depends on $\beta$ but not on the responses, so taking its <expectation> leaves it unchanged. Hence **the two updates are identical**, wherever this <Fisher information matrix> is invertible. Equivalently both solve the same <iteratively reweighted least squares> problem. This statement concerns the regression coefficients at a fixed <dispersion parameter>; a joint update of an unknown <dispersion parameter> need not have identical observed and expected full <Fisher information matrices>.

= 6B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= Solution
{parent=6B}

In the <birth-death master equation>, The upward jump has rate $\lambda$ and the downward jump rate $\beta x$, so the first two jump moments per unit time are $A(x)=\lambda-\beta x$ and $B(x)=\lambda+\beta x$. The second-order <Kramers-Moyal expansion> gives the <diffusion approximation of a birth-death process>
$$
\boxed{\partial_tP=-\partial_x[(\lambda-\beta x)P]+\frac12\partial_x^2[(\lambda+\beta x)P].}
$$
For the associated <Fokker-Planck equation>, integrating by parts with vanishing moment boundary terms gives $d\langle f\rangle/dt=\langle Af'+\tfrac12Bf''\rangle$. Taking $f=x,x^2$ yields
$$
\boxed{\frac{d\langle x\rangle}{dt}=\lambda-\beta\langle x\rangle,\qquad
\frac{d\langle x^2\rangle}{dt}=-2\beta\langle x^2\rangle+(2\lambda+\beta)\langle x\rangle+\lambda.}
$$
For example, if $m_0=\langle x(0)\rangle$, then $m(t)=\lambda/\beta+(m_0-\lambda/\beta)e^{-\beta t}$, while the <variance> satisfies $v'=-2\beta v+\lambda+\beta m$. The two boxed <moment equations> also follow exactly from the discrete <Markov jump-process generator>, since quadratic functions have no higher jump terms. A reflecting diffusion confined to $x\geq0$ can have extra boundary contributions; the formal approximation near extinction is not an exact replacement for the discrete process.

= 7E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=7E}

After division by $z$, the coefficients are $P=2/z-1$ and $Q=-1/z$. At $0$, $zP$ and $z^2Q$ are <analytic functions>, so $0$ is a <regular singular point>. Its <indicial equation> is $r(r-1)+2r=r(r+1)=0$. There are no other finite <singular points of a differential equation>. At infinity the <regular singular point criterion for a second-order equation> would require $P=O(z^{-1})$, but $P\to-1$; hence infinity is an <irregular singular point>.

Substituting $y=f/z$ reduces the equation to $f''-f'=0$, so
$$
\boxed{y(z)=\frac{A+B e^z}{z},\qquad z\ne0.}
$$
A <linearly independent> pair is $1/z$ and $(e^z-1)/z$. Their <Wronskian> is $e^z/z^2\ne0$. The second has a removable singularity at $0$ and value $1$, whereas the first has a simple pole, agreeing with the two <characteristic exponents at a regular singular point>, $0,-1$. The <integer> difference does not require a <logarithm>. At infinity $e^z/z$ has an <essential singularity>, consistent with the <irregular singular point>; the existence of the simpler solution $1/z$ does not make infinity regular.

= 8E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=8E}

<Liouville integrability> on a $2n$-dimensional <symplectic manifold> means that there are $n$ functionally independent <first integrals>, including $H$, whose pairwise <Poisson brackets> vanish. Near a <compact> connected regular common level <set>, the <Arnold-Liouville theorem> gives <action-angle variables> $(J_i,\theta_i)$ with $J_i=(2\pi)^{-1}\oint p\,dq$, $\theta_i$ periodic modulo $2\pi$, $H=H(J)$, $\dot J_i=0$ and $\dot\theta_i=\partial H/\partial J_i$.

For a frozen <mass>, the turning point is $q_*=(2E)^{1/(2k)}$. The <action variable> is
$$
 J=\frac{2}{\pi}\int_0^{q_*}\sqrt{2m(E-q^{2k}/2)}\,dq
 =C_k m^{1/2}E^{(k+1)/(2k)},\quad
 C_k=\frac{2\sqrt2\,2^{1/(2k)}}{\pi}\int_0^1\sqrt{1-s^{2k}}\,ds.
$$
<Adiabatic invariance of the action> holds to leading order when the <mass> varies slowly compared with an orbital period, away from degenerating <orbits>. Keeping $J$ fixed gives
$$
\boxed{E(t)\propto m(t)^{-k/(k+1)},\qquad c=-\frac{k}{k+1}.}
$$
For $k=1$ this agrees with $E=\omega J$ and $\omega\propto m^{-1/2}$.

= 9C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= a
{parent=9c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Chemical equilibrium> imposes $\mu_e+\mu_p=\mu_H+\mu_\gamma$. For blackbody <photons>, $\mu_\gamma=0$. Define the positive <hydrogen binding energy> by $I=(m_e+m_p-m_H)c^2$, approximately $13.6\,\mathrm{eV}$. Multiplying the two <Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution> number densities and dividing by the neutral-hydrogen density gives
$$
 \frac{n_en_p}{n_H}=\frac{g_eg_p}{g_H}
 \left(\frac{2\pi kT}{h^2}\frac{m_em_p}{m_H}\right)^{3/2}e^{-I/(kT)}.
$$
Use $g_eg_p/g_H=1$ and $m_p/m_H\simeq1$ to obtain the <Saha ionization equation>
$$
\boxed{\frac{n_en_p}{n_H}=\left(\frac{2\pi m_e kT}{h^2}\right)^{3/2}e^{-I/(kT)}.}
$$
The assumptions are nonrelativistic, dilute, nondegenerate species at a common <temperature>, ground-state hydrogen with the stated <degeneracy factors>, negligible <mass> correction in the prefactor, and sufficiently rapid reactions to maintain <chemical equilibrium>.

= b
{parent=9c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <baryon-to-photon ratio> is extremely small, of order $10^{-9}$. Thus even when the typical <photon> <energy> is below $I$, the high-<energy> tail of the <blackbody radiation> contains enough <photons> above the <ionization energy> to reionize almost every newly formed <hydrogen atom>. <Cosmological recombination> becomes effective only when this exponentially small tail is also small compared with the <baryon-to-photon ratio>. Consequently **recombination occurs at a <temperature> much lower than $I/k$**, consistent with $kT\simeq0.03I$. The exact freeze-out history also depends on reaction rates and expansion; the equilibrium estimate explains the large suppression of the <temperature>.

= 10G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= a
{parent=10g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Set $p_{-2}=0,p_{-1}=1,q_{-2}=1,q_{-1}=0$ and define the <continued fraction convergents> by
$$
 p_n=a_np_{n-1}+p_{n-2},\qquad q_n=a_nq_{n-1}+q_{n-2},\qquad
 \frac{p_n}{q_n}=[a_0;\ldots,a_n].
$$
The recurrence reverses the sign of $p_nq_{n-1}-p_{n-1}q_n$, whose initial value at $n=0$ is $-1$. Hence $p_nq_{n-1}-p_{n-1}q_n=(-1)^{n-1}$. Every common <divisor> of $p_n,q_n$ divides this number, proving $\boxed{\gcd(p_n,q_n)=1}$.

= b
{parent=10g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $D_n=p_{n-1}q_{n-2}-p_{n-2}q_{n-1}=(-1)^{n-2}$. Solving the given <Möbius transformation> for the <complete quotient> and rationalizing gives
$$
 \theta_n=\frac{p_{n-2}-\sqrt d\,q_{n-2}}{\sqrt d\,q_{n-1}-p_{n-1}}
 =\frac{D_n\sqrt d+dq_{n-1}q_{n-2}-p_{n-1}p_{n-2}}
 {p_{n-1}^2-dq_{n-1}^2}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{s_n=\frac{p_{n-1}^2-dq_{n-1}^2}{D_n},\qquad
 r_n=\frac{dq_{n-1}q_{n-2}-p_{n-1}p_{n-2}}{D_n}.}
$$
The denominator $D_n$ is $\pm1$, so both are <integers>. The <algebraic norm> denominator is nonzero because $\sqrt d$ is an <irrational number>.

= c
{parent=10g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the periodic <continued fraction> $\sqrt d=[a_0;\overline{a_1,\ldots,a_m}]$. A direct <matrix> argument avoids any assumption about the parity of the period. Let
$$
 T(a)=\begin{pmatrix}a&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\quad
 M=T(a_1)\cdots T(a_m),\quad B=T(a_0),\quad N=BMB^{-1}.
$$
Periodicity says that $M$ fixes $\theta_1$ as a <Möbius transformation>, while $B$ takes $\theta_1$ to $\sqrt d$. Thus $N=\begin{pmatrix}A&B_1\\C&D\end{pmatrix}$ has <integer> entries and fixes $\sqrt d$. Equating rational and irrational parts in $A\sqrt d+B_1=\sqrt d(C\sqrt d+D)$ gives $A=D,B_1=dC$. Consequently
$$
 A^2-dC^2=\det N=(-1)^m.
$$
The <eigenvalue> of $M$ on $(\theta_1,1)^T$ is its denominator $M_{21}\theta_1+M_{22}>1$; this uses $a_i\geq1$ and $\theta_1>1$, including the one-term period. The corresponding <eigenvalue> of $N$ is $A+C\sqrt d>1$. If the <algebraic norm> is $-1$, square this unit; otherwise keep it. In either case there is a unit $u=X+Y\sqrt d>1$ of <algebraic norm> $1$ with <integer> $X,Y$. Its powers have <integer> coefficients and <algebraic norm> $1$, and are distinct since $u^j$ strictly increases. Hence **Pell's equation has infinitely many <integer> solutions**.

= d
{parent=10g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <continued fraction convergents> of $\sqrt{67}$ begin $8,41/5,90/11,131/16,221/27$. The last one yields
$$
\boxed{x=221,\qquad y=27,\qquad 221^2-67\cdot27^2=48841-48843=-2.}
$$
Changing either sign gives another valid <integer> solution.

= 11H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Automata and formal languages}

= a
{parent=11h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <many-one reduction> $A\leq_mB$ is a <total computable function> $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ with $x\in A\iff f(x)\in B$. If $B$ is <recursively enumerable>, compute $f(x)$ and run a semidecision procedure for $B$ on that value. It halts exactly when $x\in A$, so **$A$ is <recursively enumerable>**. Totality of $f$ is essential to this argument.

= b
{parent=11h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <S-m-n theorem> says that parameters can be compiled effectively into program indices: for a standard effective numbering, there is a <total computable function> $s_m^n$ with $\varphi_{s_m^n(e,\mathbf a)}(\mathbf x)=\varphi_e(\mathbf a,\mathbf x)$ for partial computable functions of $m+n$ arguments.

Write $K=\{e:\varphi_e(e)\text{ halts}\}$, a <recursively enumerable> <halting problem>. If $X$ is <recursively enumerable>, make a program with parameter $x$ which ignores its input and runs a semidecision procedure for $x\in X$, halting when that procedure halts. By the <S-m-n theorem> its index $f(x)$ is total computable and $f(x)\in K\iff x\in X$. Conversely, $X\leq_mK$ implies that $X$ is <recursively enumerable> by the previous construction. Thus $\boxed{X\text{ is r.e.}\iff X\leq_mK}$.

= c
{parent=11h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use uniform finite-stage enumerations $W_{n,s}$ and $K_s$. Define a uniformly <recursively enumerable> <set>
$$
 V_n=\{x:\exists s\ (x\in K_s\text{ and }x<|W_{n,s}|)\}.
$$
The <S-m-n theorem> supplies a total computable index function $f$ with $W_{f(n)}=V_n$. If $W_n$ is finite of size $h$, then $V_n\subseteq\{0,\ldots,h-1\}$, so $V_n$ is <recursive>. If $W_n$ is infinite, then for every $x\in K$ a sufficiently late stage has both $x\in K_s$ and $|W_{n,s}|>x$, while no $x\notin K$ is ever enumerated. Hence $V_n=K$, which is not <recursive>. Therefore $\boxed{n\in P\iff f(n)\in Q,\quad P\leq_mQ}$. As usual the effective numbering indexes all programs; if malformed codes are included, send them to a fixed index of $K$.

= 12B
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= Solution
{parent=12B}

A fraction $1-k$ of adults survives each time step; $k$ is the adult mortality fraction. Adults produce $\mu a_n$ larvae, which mature one step later with recruitment reduced by adult-dependent competition through $1/(1+a_n)$. Eliminating larvae gives the <second-order difference equation>
$$
 a_{n+1}=(1-k)a_n+\frac{\mu a_{n-1}}{1+a_n}.
$$
At an <equilibrium point>, either $(a,b)=(0,0)$ or
$$
 \boxed{a_*=\frac\mu k-1,\qquad b_*=\mu\left(\frac\mu k-1\right),\qquad \mu>k.}
$$
Only nonnegative populations are admissible. At extinction the <Jacobian matrix> is $\begin{pmatrix}1-k&1\\\mu&0\end{pmatrix}$ and its <characteristic polynomial> is $\lambda^2-(1-k)\lambda-\mu$. Its positive root exceeds $1$ exactly when $\mu>k$. For $0<\mu<k$ both roots have modulus less than $1$; at $\mu=k$ the roots are $1,-k$, so linearization is marginal. On the nonnegative <state space> extinction is still attracting at this equality: if two consecutive adult values are bounded by $M>0$, the next is at most $(1-k)M+kM/(1+a_n)\leq M$, with strict decrease away from zero; the maximum of two consecutive adult values strictly decreases after two steps whenever it is positive; continuity on its bounded state region then excludes a positive limiting maximum. Thus the extinction <equilibrium point> is unstable exactly when the positive <equilibrium point> exists, with equality understood through nonlinear rather than strict linear stability.

At the positive <equilibrium point>, the trace and <determinant> of the <Jacobian matrix> are $T=1-2k+k^2/\mu$ and $-k$. The <Jury stability criterion> for $\lambda^2-T\lambda-k$ requires $1-T-k>0$, $1+T-k>0$ and $1-k>0$. The strict <linear stability analysis> conditions are
$$
\boxed{\mu>k,\qquad (3k-2)\mu<k^2.}
$$
For $k\leq2/3$ there is no upper bound on $\mu$; for $k>2/3$ the stable region is $k<\mu<k^2/(3k-2)$.

\Image[past-exam-of-the-mathematics-course-of-the-university-of-cambridge/2017/ii/paper-3-stability.png]
{height=550}

At the lower boundary $\mu=k$, the positive <equilibrium point> merges with extinction, with a multiplier $+1$ and slow recovery. At the upper boundary a multiplier reaches $-1$ (the other is $k$), giving alternating adult/larval fluctuations. The nonlinear map confirms a supercritical <period-doubling bifurcation>, as follows. For $k>2/3$, put $h=1-k$ and $\mu_c=k^2/(3k-2)$. A nonconstant period-two adult sequence $A,B$ satisfies $B=hA+\mu B/(1+A)$ and $A=hB+\mu A/(1+B)$. Subtracting and adding these equations gives
$$
 S=A+B=\frac{\mu-1-h}{h},\qquad P=AB=\frac{hS(S+1)}{1+h},\qquad
 (A-B)^2=\frac{S[(1-3h)S-4h]}{1+h}.
$$
Thus positive unequal values emerge for $\mu>\mu_c$, with amplitude proportional to $\sqrt{\mu-\mu_c}$. The two larval phases are $\mu B,\mu A$. If $T_2,\Delta_2$ are the trace and determinant of the two-step <Jacobian matrix>, direct multiplication gives
$$
 \Delta_2=\frac{(1+h)\mu}{S+1},\qquad
 1-T_2+\Delta_2=\frac{Sh(1+h)[(1-3h)S-4h]}{(S+1)\mu}>0.
$$
At onset $\Delta_2=k^2<1$ and $T_2=1+k^2$, so the other two strict <Jury stability criterion> inequalities hold for sufficiently small positive $\mu-\mu_c$. Hence **just beyond the upper boundary, a stable period-two population cycle replaces the stable equilibrium**. This is a local conclusion, not stability for arbitrarily large reproduction rates.

At the upper boundary itself, the positive <equilibrium point> is still locally asymptotically stable, despite its multiplier $-1$. To decide this equality case, apply the <centre manifold theorem for a discrete dynamical system>. Put $u=a-a_*$ and write the centre graph as $b-b_*=H(u)$ with $H'(0)=-\mu_c$. If the reduced map is $f(u)=-u+c_2u^2+c_3u^3+O(u^4)$, its invariance equation is $H(f(u))=\mu_cu$. Expanding the original recruitment map and this equation through cubic order gives
$$
c_2=\frac{(2-k)(3k-2)}{k(1-k)},\qquad
c_3+c_2^2=\frac{(2-k)(3k-2)^2}{k^2(k+1)}>0.
$$
Consequently $f^2(u)=u-2(c_3+c_2^2)u^3+O(u^4)$. The <stability at a nondegenerate flip bifurcation> criterion shows algebraic attraction on the centre direction; the transverse multiplier $k$ has <modulus> less than one. Including this boundary, the complete local asymptotic-stability condition for a positive population is therefore
$$
\boxed{\mu>k,\qquad (3k-2)\mu\leq k^2.}
$$
The shaded figure shows the strict linear-stability region; its upper boundary adds this nonhyperbolic attracting case.

<Demographic stochasticity> near the lower boundary can lead to absorption at extinction; near the upper boundary it excites alternating fluctuations and can blur the deterministic <period-doubling bifurcation>. These qualitative predictions depend on the chosen stochastic recruitment and mortality rules.

= 13C
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= a
{parent=13c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

With constant <masses>, differentiate the <moment of inertia> twice:
$$
 \frac12I''=\sum_i m_i\dot r_i^2+\sum_i m_i r_i\cdot\ddot r_i=2K+\sum_i r_i\cdot F_i.
$$
For each pair, $F_{ij}=-Gm_im_j(r_i-r_j)/|r_i-r_j|^3$ and $F_{ji}=-F_{ij}$. Hence its contribution is $(r_i-r_j)\cdot F_{ij}=-Gm_im_j/|r_i-r_j|=V_{ij}$. Summing over unordered pairs gives $\sum r_i\cdot F_i=V$. Time-averaging over $[0,T]$ yields
$$
 2\overline K_T+\overline V_T=\frac{I'(T)-I'(0)}{2T}.
$$
For a bound system with $I'(T)=o(T)$, or a stationary equilibrium with zero average left-hand <derivative>, the <limit> is the <virial theorem>
$$
\boxed{2\langle K\rangle+\langle V\rangle=0.}
$$
Bounded spatial extent together with bounded <velocities> suffices for the <derivative> condition; possible gravitational collisions must be excluded or otherwise regularized.

= b
{parent=13c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the initially homogeneous <sphere>, $V_i=-3GM^2/(5R_i)$ and $K_i=0$. <Conservation of energy> and the <virial theorem> imply $E_f=V_f/2=V_i$, hence $V_f=2V_i$. With the usual homologous-<sphere> approximation, $V\propto-1/R$, so
$$
\boxed{R_f=R_i/2,\qquad \rho_f=8\rho_i.}
$$
These are the conventional collapse estimates. A final virial equilibrium is not, by itself, guaranteed to remain homogeneous: if $V_f=-\alpha_f GM^2/R_f$, the actual relation is $R_f/R_i=\alpha_f/(2\alpha_i)$ with $\alpha_i=3/5$. Thus the factor of two applies exactly to the gravitational radius, and to the outer radius only under the stated structural approximation.

Here “non-interacting” means collisionless apart from gravity. Baryonic gas can collide, convert ordered motion to thermal <energy>, and radiate it. If <radiative cooling> is effective, the fixed-<energy> virial state loses <pressure> support and contracts further. **Baryonic cooling can destabilize the collisionless virial state**; baryons are not inevitably unstable if cooling is slow or heating and <pressure> support maintain equilibrium.

= 14H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Logic and Set Theory}

= Solution
{parent=14H}

<Zorn lemma> states that a <partially ordered set> in which every <chain in a partially ordered set> has an upper bound has a <maximal element>. Include the empty <chain in a partially ordered set>, so the hypotheses imply that the <set> $P$ is nonempty. Suppose there were no <maximal element>. Every <chain in a partially ordered set> $C$ has an upper bound $u$, and some $v>u$; therefore the <set> of elements strictly above every member of $C$ is nonempty. The <axiom of choice> selects one such element $f(C)$ for each <chain in a partially ordered set> (and an element of $P$ when $C$ is empty).

By <Hartogs theorem>, there is an <ordinal> $\kappa$ which does not inject into $P$. <Transfinite recursion> defines $x_\alpha=f(\{x_\beta:\beta<\alpha\})$ for all $\alpha<\kappa$: previous elements form a strictly increasing <chain in a partially ordered set>, including at <limit ordinal> stages. All $x_\alpha$ are distinct, giving the forbidden injection. This proves <Zorn lemma>; **the choice of $f$ is the use of the <axiom of choice>**.

Apply <Zorn lemma> to <linearly independent> <subsets> of $\mathbb R$ over $\mathbb Q$, ordered by inclusion. A <union> along a <chain in a partially ordered set> is <linearly independent> because each finite linear relation occurs in one member of that <chain in a partially ordered set>. A maximal independent <set> spans $\mathbb R$, since a <vector> outside its span could be adjoined. Thus **$\mathbb R$ has a Hamel <basis> over $\mathbb Q$**.

For a finite <basis>, the <Steinitz exchange lemma> proves equality of <basis> sizes. This also rules out an infinite <basis> when a finite spanning <set> exists: write each member of the finite spanning <set> using finitely many elements of another <basis>; their finite <union> spans, and independence leaves no other <basis> elements. If a <basis> $B$ is infinite with <cardinality> $\lambda$, the finite-support description of every <vector> and countability of $\mathbb Q$ give
$$
 \lambda\leq|V|\leq\sum_{n<\omega}|B|^n|\mathbb Q|^n=\lambda.
$$
Here infinite <cardinal arithmetic> uses the <axiom of choice>. Thus any infinite <basis> has <cardinality> $|V|$, proving **all <bases> have the same <cardinality>**. The zero space has its unique empty <basis>.

= 15H
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= Solution
{parent=15H}

A <graph Ramsey number> $R(s,t)$ is the least $N$ such that every red-blue edge colouring of the <complete graph> $K_N$ has a red $K_s$ or a blue $K_t$. At a vertex in $K_{R(s-1,t)+R(s,t-1)}$, either at least $R(s-1,t)$ neighbours have red incident edges or at least $R(s,t-1)$ have blue incident edges. In the first case find a red $K_{s-1}$ and adjoin the vertex, or a blue $K_t$; the second case is symmetric. Thus
$$
 R(s,t)\leq R(s-1,t)+R(s,t-1).
$$
Starting with $R(2,t)=t$ and $R(s,2)=s$, induction gives the <binomial upper bound for a Ramsey number>
$$
\boxed{R(s,t)\leq\binom{s+t-2}{s-1},\qquad R(s,s)\leq\binom{2s-2}{s-1}\leq4^{s-1}<4^s.}
$$
For the lower construction, partition $2t-2$ vertices into two classes of size $t-1$, colour inside-class edges red and between-class edges blue. Red <cliques> have at most $t-1$ vertices, and the blue <graph> is <bipartite>, so has no <odd cycle>.

Conversely, a <graph> without an <odd cycle> is <bipartite>: in each connected component colour vertices by the parity of the length of a path from a root; differing path parities would give an odd closed walk, hence an <odd cycle>. On $2t-1$ vertices one of the two blue bipartition classes has at least $t$ vertices. Every edge within it is red, giving a red $K_t$. Thus **$2t-1$ is the exact threshold** for this alternative.

= 16I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= a
{parent=16i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $|F|=p^n$, where $n=[F:\mathbb F_p]$ since $F$ is a finite-dimensional <vector space> over its <prime field>. Every nonzero element satisfies $x^{p^n-1}=1$ by <Lagrange theorem>, so $F$ is exactly the <splitting field> of $X^{p^n}-X$ over $\mathbb F_p$. The <derivative> is $-1$, making it a <separable polynomial>. Thus the extension is a <normal field extension> and a <separable field extension>, hence a <Galois extension>.

The <finite-field Frobenius automorphism> $\sigma(x)=x^p$ fixes $\mathbb F_p$ and has $\sigma^n=1$. If $\sigma^d=1$ with $0<d<n$, all $p^n$ elements would be roots of the degree-$p^d$ <polynomial> $X^{p^d}-X$, impossible. It has order $n$, equal to the degree of the <Galois extension>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}(F/\mathbb F_p)=\langle\sigma\rangle\cong C_n.}
$$

= b
{parent=16i}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Over $\mathbb F_3$,
$$
 t^4+1=(t^2+t+2)(t^2+2t+2).
$$
Both quadratics have <discriminant> $2$, a nonsquare in $\mathbb F_3$, so are <irreducible polynomials>. Both split in the same quadratic <finite field> $\mathbb F_9$. Consequently $\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}(t^4+1/\mathbb F_3)\cong C_2}$. Its generator is the <finite-field Frobenius automorphism> $x\mapsto x^3$, acting as a product of two transpositions on the four roots.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <polynomial> $t^3-t-2$ has values $3,3,4,2,3$ at $0,1,2,3,4$ respectively, modulo $5$. Thus $t^3-t-2$ has no root and is an <irreducible polynomial> over $\mathbb F_5$. One root generates $\mathbb F_{125}$; all its <Frobenius conjugates> are there, so this is its <splitting field>. Hence $\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}(t^3-t-2/\mathbb F_5)\cong C_3}$, generated by $x\mapsto x^5$, a 3-<cycle> on the roots.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Over $\mathbb F_7$, the <polynomial> $t^4-1$ factors as $(t-1)(t+1)(t^2+1)$. Since $-1$ is not a square modulo $7$, the last factor is an <irreducible polynomial>. The <splitting field> is $\mathbb F_{49}$, so $\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}(t^4-1/\mathbb F_7)\cong C_2}$. The <finite-field Frobenius automorphism> fixes $1,-1$ and swaps the other two roots.

= 17G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= a
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Burnside's theorem> states that **every finite <group> of order $p^a q^b$ is solvable**, where $p,q$ are <primes> and $a,b$ are nonnegative <integers>. In particular, a nonabelian <simple group> cannot have order divisible by only one or two distinct <primes>.

= b
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Conjugation> by $P$ partitions $H$ into <orbits>. Each non-singleton <orbit> has size divisible by $p$, by the <orbit-stabilizer theorem>. The singleton <orbits> are exactly $H\cap Z(P)$. Since $|H|$ is divisible by $p$, $|H\cap Z(P)|\equiv0\pmod p$. This <set> contains $1$, so $\boxed{|H\cap Z(P)|\geq p}$.

For the deduction we also give the representation-theoretic ingredient behind <Burnside's theorem>: a nonabelian finite <simple group> has no nontrivial <conjugacy class> of <prime power> size. Here is a proof, so no stronger theorem is being assumed without justification. Suppose $g\ne1$ has class size $p^b$. For an <irreducible character> $\chi$ of degree $d$ coprime to $p$, the <class sum> acts as the scalar $p^b\chi(g)/d$, which is an <algebraic integer>: the <class sum> has an integer-entry <matrix> in the <regular representation>, which contains every <irreducible representation>, so this scalar is an eigenvalue of an integer-entry matrix. Also $\chi(g)$ is an <algebraic integer>. Bezout's identity then makes $\chi(g)/d$ an <algebraic integer>. All its <algebraic conjugates> have modulus at most $1$, because <character values of a representation> are sums of $d$ <roots of unity>. An <algebraic integer> with this property is zero or a <root of unity>: the monic <polynomials> of all its powers have uniformly bounded <integer> coefficients, so two powers must coincide unless it is zero.

Every nontrivial <irreducible representation> of a <simple group> is faithful. If $\chi(g)/d$ were a <root of unity>, equality in the triangle inequality would force its representing <matrix> to be scalar, hence $g$ central, impossible. Thus $\chi(g)=0$ whenever $p\nmid d$, except for the trivial <character of a representation>. Orthogonality of the identity column and the $g$ column in the <character table> now gives
$$
 0=1+\sum_{p\mid\chi(1)}\chi(1)\overline{\chi(g)}.
$$
This would make $1/p$ an <algebraic integer>, contradicting the fact that rational <algebraic integers> are <integers>. The same proof covers class size $1$ directly through triviality of the center.

If an abelian <subgroup> $A$ had index $p^a$, every $g\in A\setminus\{1\}$ would have $A\subseteq C_G(g)$, making its class size a <divisor> of $p^a$, a contradiction. Such a $g$ exists: if $A=1$, then $G$ is a nontrivial <p-group>, whose center is nontrivial by the first argument with $H=P$. Therefore **a nonabelian <simple group> has no abelian <subgroup> of <prime power> index**.

= c
{parent=17g}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Multiplicativity of the <determinant> gives $\delta(gh)=\delta(g)\delta(h)$ and $\delta(1)=1$, so $\delta$ is a <linear character>. Its finite image is a cyclic <group> of <roots of unity>. If it contains $-1$, its order is $2m$ and the <character of a representation> $\delta^m$ maps onto $\{\pm1\}$. The <kernel of a group homomorphism> is the preimage of its identity, so $\ker(\delta^m)$ is a <normal subgroup> of index $2$. The <subgroup> $\ker\delta$ itself need not have index $2$.

By <Cauchy theorem for groups>, a <group> $E$ of order $2k$ has an involution $g$. In the <regular representation>, left multiplication by $g$ is a permutation consisting of $k$ disjoint transpositions, so its <determinant> is $(-1)^k=-1$ when $k$ is odd. The preceding argument gives **a <normal subgroup> of index two**.

For a nonabelian <simple group> of order below $80$, <Burnside's theorem> requires at least three distinct <prime> factors. An odd order with three factors is at least $3\cdot5\cdot7=105$. An even order with exactly one factor of $2$ has a <normal subgroup> of index $2$. Thus its order must be divisible by $4$ and by at least two odd <primes>. Below $80$ the only possibility is $4\cdot3\cdot5=60$, since the next is $4\cdot3\cdot7=84$. Hence $\boxed{|H|=60}$.

= 18I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= Solution
{parent=18I}

Use <singular homology> with <integer> coefficients. The <Kunneth theorem> for triangulable spaces gives a split short exact <sequence> with tensor terms $\bigoplus_{i+j=k}H_i(X)\otimes H_j(Y)$ and torsion terms $\bigoplus_{i+j=k-1}\operatorname{Tor}_1(H_i(X),H_j(Y))$. When one factor's <homology groups> are free abelian, the torsion terms vanish and the tensor map is an <isomorphism>.

The <circle> has $H_0(S^1)=H_1(S^1)=\mathbb Z$ and all other <groups> zero. Inductively, the <homology groups> of a <torus> are free, and
$$
 H_k(T^{n-1}\times S^1)\cong H_k(T^{n-1})\oplus H_{k-1}(T^{n-1}).
$$
Pascal's identity gives
$$
\boxed{H_k(T^n;\mathbb Z)\cong\mathbb Z^{\binom nk}\quad(0\leq k\leq n),\qquad H_k=0\quad(k>n).}
$$
Geometrically the generators correspond to choosing $k$ of the $n$ <circle> factors and taking their product, consistent with the cellular model having one $k$-cell for each such <subset> and cancelling opposite boundary faces.

= 19F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= i
{parent=19f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The real <Stone-Weierstrass theorem> says that a real <subalgebra> $A\subset C(K,\mathbb R)$ which contains the constants and separates points is dense in $C(K,\mathbb R)$ for the <uniform norm>, when $K$ is <compact Hausdorff space>. If $A$ is also closed, **$A=C(K,\mathbb R)$**. The complex version needs closure under <complex conjugation>; that extra condition is unnecessary here.

= ii
{parent=19f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Since a <subalgebra> is closed under addition and scalar multiplication, the given absolute-value property yields
$$
 \boxed{\max(f,g)=\frac{f+g+|f-g|}{2}\in A.}
$$
The same identity with a minus sign gives $\min(f,g)\in A$. These are pointwise operations on real-valued <continuous functions>.

= iii
{parent=19f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $F,G$ be disjoint closed <subsets> of $K$. They are <compact>. For each $x\in F,y\in G$, the <Hausdorff space> condition gives disjoint open neighbourhoods $U_{xy},V_{xy}$. For fixed $x$, finitely many $V_{xy}$ cover $G$; intersect the corresponding $U_{xy}$ and take the <union> of those $V_{xy}$ to obtain disjoint open <sets> $U_x\ni x$ and $V_x\supset G$. Finitely many $U_x$ cover $F$. Their <union> and the <intersection> of the corresponding $V_x$ are disjoint open neighbourhoods of $F,G$. The case of an empty <set> is immediate. Hence **$K$ is a <normal topological space>**.

<Urysohn lemma> states that in a <normal topological space> satisfying $T_1$, disjoint closed <sets> $F,G$ admit a <continuous function> $h:K\to[0,1]$ with $h=0$ on $F$ and $h=1$ on $G$.

= iv
{parent=19f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For $x\ne y$, $|(\delta_x-\delta_y)(f)|\leq2\|f\|_\infty$, giving an upper bound $2$ for the <operator norm>. By <Urysohn lemma>, there is $h:K\to[0,1]$ with $h(x)=1,h(y)=0$. Then $f=2h-1$ has <uniform norm> $1$ and $(\delta_x-\delta_y)(f)=2$. Thus every distinct pair has <operator norm> $2$, and
$$
\boxed{\inf_{x\ne y}\|\delta_x-\delta_y\|=2\quad\text{if }|K|\geq2.}
$$
The stated hypotheses also permit a singleton $K$. Then there are no distinct pairs, so the infimum is **$+\infty$ under the extended-real convention**, or undefined as a real number. This edge case cannot be assigned the value $2$.

= 20F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Analysis of Functions}

= i
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Dominated convergence makes $\widehat f$ <continuous>, and $\|\widehat f\|_\infty\leq\|f\|_1$. Smooth compactly supported functions are dense in $L^1$. Their <Fourier transforms> decay at infinity by <integration by parts>, so uniform approximation proves the <Riemann-Lebesgue lemma>: $\widehat f\in C_0$.

For density, smooth compactly supported functions are also uniformly dense in $C_0$ by cutoff and mollification. Each such target $g$ is a <Schwartz function>; its inverse <Fourier transform> is another <Schwartz function>, hence belongs to $L^1$, and <Fourier inversion> recovers $g$. Therefore the <image of a function> of $L^1$ is dense in $C_0$.

The $L^1$ <Fourier inversion theorem> says that if $f,\widehat f\in L^1$, then $f$ agrees almost everywhere with the inverse <integral> of $\widehat f$. If $\widehat f=0$, this applies and gives $f=0$ almost everywhere. Hence **the <Fourier transform> is <injective> as a map of $L^1$ equivalence classes, with dense image in $C_0$**.

= ii
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Integrating the exponential gives the <Fourier transform of an interval indicator>
$$
\boxed{\widehat{\chi_{[a,b]}}(\xi)=\frac{e^{-2\pi i a\xi}-e^{-2\pi i b\xi}}{2\pi i\xi}\quad(\xi\ne0),\qquad \widehat{\chi_{[a,b]}}(0)=b-a.}
$$
The value at zero is the removable <limit>, for $a\leq b$.

= iii
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $n\geq1$, the <convolution> equals the length of the overlap of two intervals:
$$
 g_n(\xi)=\begin{cases}2,&|\xi|\leq n-1,\\n+1-|\xi|,&n-1<|\xi|<n+1,\\0,&|\xi|\geq n+1.\end{cases}
$$
It is <continuous> and compactly supported, with $\|g_n\|_\infty=2$. Inverse <Fourier transform> turns this <convolution> into a product, so
$$
\boxed{h_n(x)=\frac{\sin(2\pi n x)\sin(2\pi x)}{\pi^2x^2}\ (x\ne0),\qquad h_n(0)=4n.}
$$
This function is bounded near zero and $O(x^{-2})$ at infinity, hence lies in $L^1$. <Fourier inversion>, or the <convolution theorem> applied first to the integrable interval indicators, gives $\widehat h_n=g_n$.

= iv
{parent=20f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

If the bounded <injective> <Fourier transform> $L^1\to C_0$ were surjective, the <open mapping theorem> for <Banach spaces> would make its inverse bounded, yielding $\|h\|_1\leq C\|\widehat h\|_\infty$. But the preceding $g_n$ have <uniform norm> $2$, whereas $\|h_n\|_1$ diverges.

For a quantitative lower bound, on $0<x\leq1/4$ we have $\sin(2\pi x)\geq4x$. On each interval $I_j=[(j+1/12)/n,(j+5/12)/n]$, $|\sin(2\pi n x)|\geq1/2$. For $1\leq j\leq\lfloor n/8\rfloor$ these intervals lie below $1/4$ for large $n$, so
$$
 \|h_n\|_1\geq\frac2{\pi^2}\sum_j\int_{I_j}\frac{dx}{x}
 \geq\frac{2}{3\pi^2}\sum_{j=1}^{\lfloor n/8\rfloor}\frac1{j+5/12}\longrightarrow\infty.
$$
This contradicts boundedness of the inverse. Therefore **the <Fourier transform> has proper dense image in $C_0(\mathbb R)$**.

= 21F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Riemann Surfaces}

= Solution
{parent=21F}

Set $F(z,w)=w^2-z^n+1$. Its complex <gradient> is $(-nz^{n-1},2w)$, never zero on $F=0$: simultaneous zero would give $z=w=0$, which is not on the <curve>. The complex <implicit function theorem> therefore gives a one-dimensional complex <manifold>, hence a <Riemann surface>. Where $w\ne0$ use $z$ as coordinate, and where $z\ne0$ use $w$; the transition maps and both projections are <holomorphic>.

For $\pi$, a <ramification point of a holomorphic map> has $w=0$ and $z=\zeta$ with $\zeta^n=1$. In the coordinate $w$, $z-\zeta=w^2/(n\zeta^{n-1})+O(w^4)$, giving <ramification index> $2$. Thus there are $n$ such points, and their <branch points> are the $n$th <roots of unity>.

For $\tau$, a <ramification point of a holomorphic map> has $z=0,w=\pm i$. In the coordinate $z$, $w-w_0=z^n/(2w_0)+O(z^{2n})$, giving <ramification index> $n$ at both points and <branch points> $\pm i$.

Write $n=2m$. At infinity set $u=1/z$, $v=w u^m$; then $v^2=1-u^n$. At $u=0$ there are two smooth points, $v=\pm1$, and $u$ is a local coordinate. The extended $\pi$ has a simple pole at each, so neither is ramified. The degree-two <Riemann-Hurwitz formula> for the map to the <Riemann sphere> gives $2g-2=2(-2)+n$, hence
$$
\boxed{g=\frac{n-2}{2}.}
$$
The list for $\tau$ above concerns the original affine <Riemann surface>; on the compactification its poles at the two added points have order $m$ and are additionally ramified if $m>1$.

= 22I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Geometry}

= a
{parent=22i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For <algebraic varieties> that are <irreducible topological spaces>, a <rational map> $X\dashrightarrow Y$ is an equivalence class of <morphisms> $U\to Y$ on nonempty open <subsets> of $X$, with two representatives equivalent if they agree on a nonempty open <intersection>. Such <subsets> are dense. **A <birational map> has a rational inverse**; equivalently it restricts to an <isomorphism> between dense open <subsets>. In <function field> language it induces an <isomorphism> $k(Y)\cong k(X)$ over the ground <field>.

= b
{parent=22i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Work over the usual algebraically closed ground <field> of characteristic zero. Away from $x=0$, a line of slope $t=y/x$ through the singular point meets the <curve> with $t^2=x-1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\phi(x,y)=y/x,\qquad \psi(t)=(t^2+1,\ t(t^2+1)).}
$$
Substitution verifies the defining equation. On $x\ne0$ and $t^2+1\ne0$, the two maps are inverse <morphisms>, so this is a <birational map> to $\mathbb A^1$. The two parameters $t=\pm i$ both map to the node $(0,0)$, explaining why the globally defined parametrization is not a global <isomorphism>.

= c
{parent=22i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Let $F(y)=y_1^2y_2+y_2^2y_3+y_3^2y_1$. The <proper transform> is
$$
\boxed{\widetilde Y=\{(x,[y]):x_iy_j=x_jy_i\ (i<j),\ F(y)=0\}\subset\mathbb A^3\times\mathbb P^2.}
$$
Indeed, in the blowup chart $y_i\ne0$, write $x=t y$ with $y_i=1$. Homogeneity gives $F(x)=t^3F(y)$. Away from the <exceptional divisor> $t=0$, the equation is $F(y)=0$, and its closure retains that equation, removing the exceptional factor $t^3$. Every point with $t=0,F(y)=0$ is approached algebraically along the line $(t y,[y])$, so no component in this displayed <set> is extraneous.

For $x\ne0$, the fibre is the single point $[y]=[x]$, and the projection is an <isomorphism> over $Y\setminus\{0\}$. Over $0$ the fibre is the projective plane cubic $F(y)=0$. In characteristic zero this cubic is smooth: a coordinate zero in the three <derivative> equations would force all coordinates zero; with all nonzero, multiplying $2y_1y_2=-y_3^2$, $y_1^2=-2y_2y_3$, $y_2^2=-2y_3y_1$ gives $1=-8$, impossible. Thus **the exceptional fibre is a smooth <genus>-one plane cubic**. The fibre description itself does not require this optional smoothness conclusion; characteristic $3$ requires separate treatment.

= 23I
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= Solution
{parent=23I}

The <length of a curve> is $L(\alpha)=\int_a^b\|\alpha'(t)\|dt$. Its <arc length> parameter is $s(t)=\int_a^t\|\alpha'(u)\|du$. Regularity gives $s'(t)>0$, so the inverse function theorem gives a smooth inverse and $\beta(s)=\alpha(t(s))$ has $\|\beta'(s)\|=1$.

Reparametrize a length minimizer to constant speed on $[a,b]$ and use the <energy functional> $E=\tfrac12\int_a^b\|\alpha'\|^2dt$. By <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>, every competitor has $E\geq L^2/(2(b-a))$, with equality for constant speed, so this parametrization also minimizes $E$. For a fixed-endpoint <variation> with tangential <vector field> $V$, differentiation and <integration by parts> give
$$
 \delta E=\int_a^b\langle\alpha',V'\rangle dt
 =-\int_a^b\langle\alpha'',V\rangle dt
 =-\int_a^b\langle\nabla_t\alpha',V\rangle dt.
$$
The boundary term is zero. Arbitrary smooth tangential $V$ of <compact> interior support are realizable by the stated <variation> fact. Taking $V$ to be a nonnegative cutoff times $\nabla_t\alpha'$ proves $\nabla_t\alpha'=0$. Hence **a length minimizer is a <geodesic> after constant-speed reparametrization**. The original parameter need not be affine: arbitrary varying-speed parametrizations preserve length but have nonzero covariant <acceleration>. This qualification is necessary for the literal wording.

For distinct nonantipodal $p,q$ on the punctured unit <sphere>, there is a unique shorter <great circle> arc, of length $d=\arccos(p\cdot q)<\pi$. A minimizing <curve> exists **exactly when that shorter arc avoids the removed north pole**. If it avoids the pole it attains the spherical lower bound. If it passes through the pole, arbitrarily small smooth detours have lengths tending to $d$, but equality would force the unique shorter arc, which is unavailable. Thus the infimum is not attained; the longer <great circle> arc is not a substitute minimizer.

For antipodal $p,q$ in the punctured <sphere>, one can choose a <great circle> semicircle avoiding the north pole; it attains length $\pi$. Thus **every admissible antipodal pair has a minimizer**. If identical endpoints are included despite the earlier distinctness stipulation, the infimum is zero, but no smooth regular <curve> attains it; only a nonregular constant <curve> does.

= 24J
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= a
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A family $(X_n)$ of <integrable random variables> is <uniformly integrable> when
$$
\boxed{\lim_{M\to\infty}\sup_n\mathbb E\bigl[|X_n|\mathbf1_{\{|X_n|>M\}}\bigr]=0.}
$$
Equivalently the <integrals> of their tails vanish uniformly. This includes uniform $L^1$ boundedness, since $\mathbb E|X_n|\leq M+\mathbb E[|X_n|\mathbf1_{|X_n|>M}]$.

= b
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For conjugate exponents $1<p,q<\infty$, $1/p+1/q=1$, <Hölder's inequality> is $\boxed{\mathbb E|XY|\leq\|X\|_p\|Y\|_q}$. If either finite <norm> is zero, the corresponding variable is zero <almost surely>. Otherwise normalize $u=|X|/\|X\|_p$, $v=|Y|/\|Y\|_q$. <Young inequality> $uv\leq u^p/p+v^q/q$ follows by minimizing the right side minus $uv$ in $u\geq0$, whose <derivative> is $u^{p-1}-v$. Integration gives $\mathbb E(uv)\leq1/p+1/q=1$, proving the result. Infinite <norms> give the usual extended-value inequality whenever the right side is unambiguous. At the endpoints, $\mathbb E|XY|\leq\|X\|_1\|Y\|_\infty$ follows from the almost-sure bound for $Y$, with the symmetric version for $p=\infty$.

= c
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

$L^p$ boundedness means $\sup_n\|X_n\|_p<\infty$, with the essential-supremum <norm> for $p=\infty$. For finite $p>1$, let $C=\sup_n\mathbb E|X_n|^p<\infty$. On $|X_n|>M$, $|X_n|\leq M^{1-p}|X_n|^p$, so
$$
 \boxed{\sup_n\mathbb E[|X_n|\mathbf1_{|X_n|>M}]\leq C M^{1-p}\longrightarrow0.}
$$
This proves <uniform integrability>. For an $L^\infty$ bounded family the tails are identically zero beyond the common essential bound.

= d
{parent=24j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The assertion is **false**. On $([0,1],\mathcal B,\text{Lebesgue measure})$, put $X_n=n\mathbf1_{(0,1/n)}$. Then $\mathbb E|X_n|=1$ for every $n$, so the family is $L^1$ bounded. For every $M$, choose $n>M$; then $\mathbb E[|X_n|\mathbf1_{|X_n|>M}]=1$. The tail criterion for <uniform integrability> fails.

= 25K
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability}

= a
{parent=25k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In a neutral <Moran model>, a population has $N$ labelled individuals; reproduction copies one individual's type to a uniformly chosen other individual, keeping population size fixed. One convenient time convention puts independent rate-$1/2$ Poisson arrows on each ordered pair of distinct individuals. Backwards in time, two sampled ancestral lineages merge whenever one copies the other, at total rate $1$ per unordered pair. In the convention where each individual reproduces at rate $1$, this pair rate is $2/(N-1)$ if the parent cannot replace itself, and time must be rescaled accordingly.

<Kingman's coalescent> on $n$ labels is the <continuous-time Markov chain> on partitions of $\{1,\ldots,n\}$, initially all singleton blocks, in which each pair of distinct blocks merges at rate $1$. With $j$ blocks, the waiting time is exponential of rate $\binom j2$, and the merging pair is uniform. <Kingman's infinite coalescent> is the consistent partition process on $\mathbb N$ whose restriction to each finite label <set> has this law; consistency and the extension theorem construct it. These time conventions are part of the definition.

Let $B_t^{(n)}$ be the number of blocks in the first $n$ labels. The expected time to reach at most $m$ blocks is
$$
 \mathbb E T_m^{(n)}=\sum_{j=m+1}^n\binom j2^{-1}=2\left(\frac1m-\frac1n\right)\leq\frac2m.
$$
<Markov inequality> gives $\mathbb P(B_t^{(n)}>m)\leq2/(mt)$. As $n$ increases these counts increase to the total number of infinite-coalescent blocks, so the same bound holds for that number. Letting $m\to\infty$ shows it is finite <almost surely> for each $t>0$. Apply this simultaneously to positive rational times; monotonicity of the block count then covers every positive real time. Hence **<Kingman's infinite coalescent> comes down from infinity <almost surely>**.

= b
{parent=25k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

A <renewal process> has independent identically distributed, nonnegative, <almost surely> finite inter-arrival times $X_i$, epochs $S_0=0,S_n=\sum_{i=1}^nX_i$, and count $N(t)=\max\{n:S_n\leq t\}$. Allowing zero inter-arrivals is harmless provided $\mathbb P(X_1>0)>0$. The hypothesis $\mathbb E X_1>0$ ensures there is $\varepsilon>0$ with $p=\mathbb P(X_1\geq\varepsilon)>0$.

The <strong law of large numbers> for the indicators gives $S_n\geq\varepsilon\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf1_{X_i\geq\varepsilon}\to\infty$, so $N(t)$ is finite for finite $t$. Conversely every fixed $S_n$ is finite, so eventually $N(t)\geq n$. On their countable common <probability>-one event, this proves $\boxed{N(t)\to\infty\text{ as }t\to\infty\text{ almost surely}}$.

For fixed $t$, put $L=\lfloor t/\varepsilon\rfloor$. The hinted comparison process has epochs $S_n'=\varepsilon\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf1_{X_i\geq\varepsilon}\leq S_n$, hence $N(t)\leq N'(t)$. Equivalently,
$$
 \mathbb P(N(t)\geq n)\leq\mathbb P(\operatorname{Bin}(n,p)\leq L).
$$
If $p<1$, for $n>L$ this is bounded by $C_{t,p} n^L(1-p)^n$, by summing the first $L+1$ <binomial distribution>. Thus $\sum_ne^{\theta n}\mathbb P(N(t)=n)<\infty$ for $0<\theta<-\log(1-p)$. If $p=1$, $N(t)\leq L$ deterministically. Therefore **a strictly positive exponential moment exists** for every fixed finite $t$, with the same allowed $\theta$ for all such $t$. Almost-sure finiteness of the inter-arrivals is implicit in a proper <renewal process>; allowing an infinite inter-arrival with positive <probability> would invalidate the divergence claim.

= 26K
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= a
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <score function> is
$$
 S_n(\theta)=\frac{d}{d\theta}\log\prod_{i=1}^nf(X_i,\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^n\frac{\partial_\theta f(X_i,\theta)}{f(X_i,\theta)}.
$$
With common parameter-independent support and justified differentiation under the <integral>, $\mathbb E_\theta[\partial_\theta\log f(X_1,\theta)]=\int\partial_\theta f(x,\theta)dx=\partial_\theta1=0$. Linearity then gives $\boxed{\mathbb E_{\theta_0}S_n(\theta_0)=0}$. These regularity conditions exclude, for example, moving-support uniform models.

= b
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The per-observation <Fisher information> is $I(\theta)=\mathbb E_\theta[(\partial_\theta\log f(X_1,\theta))^2]$. Since
$$
 \partial_\theta^2\log f=\frac{f_{\theta\theta}}f-\left(\frac{f_\theta}f\right)^2,
$$
and the <integral> of $f_{\theta\theta}$ is the second <derivative> of $1$, the regularity assumptions give
$$
\boxed{I(\theta)=-\mathbb E_\theta[\partial_\theta^2\log f(X_1,\theta)].}
$$
For independent observations the total <Fisher information> is $nI(\theta)$, because centered individual <score functions> have zero cross terms.

= c
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <maximum likelihood estimate> $\hat\theta_n$ is a measurable maximizer of $\ell_n(\theta)=\sum_i\log f(X_i,\theta)$ over $\Theta$. Under identifiability, consistency conditions, an interior true parameter, sufficient differentiability and finite positive <Fisher information>, the standard <limits> are
$$
\boxed{\hat\theta_n\xrightarrow{\mathbb P}\theta_0,\qquad
 \sqrt n(\hat\theta_n-\theta_0)\xrightarrow{d}\mathcal N(0,I(\theta_0)^{-1}).}
$$
The <variance> in the <normal distribution> is the reciprocal of the per-observation <Fisher information>, not of $nI$. Strong consistency needs additional hypotheses if it is to replace <convergence in probability>; the regular asymptotic result stated here does not require claiming it.

= d
{parent=26k}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The almost-sure bound gives $\tilde\theta_n-\hat\theta_n=o_{\mathbb P}(n^{-1/2})$, so <Slutsky theorem> transfers both consistency and the asymptotic normal law to $\tilde\theta_n$. The <continuous mapping theorem> and <delta method> then give
$$
\boxed{\psi(\tilde\theta_n)\xrightarrow{\mathbb P}\psi(\theta_0),\qquad
\sqrt n\bigl(\psi(\tilde\theta_n)-\psi(\theta_0)\bigr)
 \xrightarrow{d}\mathcal N\!\left(0,\frac{\psi'(\theta_0)^2}{I(\theta_0)}\right).}
$$
If $\psi'(\theta_0)=0$, the latter is the <Dirac measure> at zero, conventionally a degenerate <normal distribution>; a nondegenerate second-order <limit> would need a different normalization and extra smoothness.

= 27J
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= a
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a finite-state, finite-horizon discrete-time frictionless market with a strictly positive <numeraire>, the <fundamental theorem of asset pricing> states **no arbitrage if and only if there exists an <equivalent martingale measure>** under which all discounted asset prices are <martingales>. Equivalence means the same null events as the physical measure. In this setting <market completeness> is equivalent to uniqueness of the <equivalent martingale measure>, and a replicable payoff $H$ has price $S_t^0\mathbb E_Q[H/S_T^0\mid\mathcal F_t]$. Without finite-state hypotheses, stronger integrability and closure conditions are needed; this is the standard multi-period course version.

= b
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The difference of the terminal <European call option> and <European put option> payoffs is $S_T^1-K$. Replicating that difference by one share and a borrowing position gives conditional <put-call parity>
$$
 c_{t_0}-p_{t_0}=S_{t_0}^1-K(1+r)^{t_0-T}.
$$
Thus the call is more valuable exactly when the right side is positive. With a put selected at equality, the <chooser option> payoff is
$$
\boxed{H=(S_T^1-K)^+\mathbf1_{\{S_{t_0}^1>K(1+r)^{t_0-T}\}}
 +(K-S_T^1)^+\mathbf1_{\{S_{t_0}^1\leq K(1+r)^{t_0-T}\}}.}
$$
At equality both choices have the same price, even though their eventual payoffs can differ. <Put-call parity> assumes no dividends or other intermediate stock cash flows, as in the stated asset-price model.

= c
{parent=27j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $K'=K(1+r)^{t_0-T}$. At the choice time,
$$
 \max(c_{t_0},p_{t_0})=c_{t_0}+(K'-S_{t_0}^1)^+.
$$
Buy a <European call option> of strike $K$ and maturity $T$, together with a <European put option> of strike $K'$ and maturity $t_0$. If the call is selected, the short-maturity put expires worthless. If the put is selected, sell the call and combine its value with the short-maturity put's cash payment; <put-call parity> makes this exactly enough to buy the maturity-$T$ put. This self-financing replication proves
$$
\boxed{\pi(C(K,t_0,T))=\pi(EC(K,T))+\pi(EP(K(1+r)^{t_0-T},t_0)).}
$$
It requires tradability of the priced options at the choice time, but not completeness of every other market claim. The identity also covers $t_0=0$ and $t_0=T$.

= 28K
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Optimization and Control}

= Solution
{parent=28K}

The stochastic objective is interpreted as its <expectation>; a literal pathwise minimization is generally impossible without knowing future noises. Let the value function from time $t$ be $V_t(x)=P_t x^2+d_t$, with $P_T=1,d_T=0$. Independence from past observations and zero contemporaneous <covariance> give
$$
 \mathbb E[x_{t+1}^2\mid x_t=x,u_t=u]=V_\xi(Ax+u)^2+V_\epsilon.
$$
The <Bellman equation> is $V_t(x)=x^2+\min_u\{cu^2+P_{t+1}V_\xi(Ax+u)^2+P_{t+1}V_\epsilon+d_{t+1}\}$. Completing the square yields
$$
\boxed{u_t^*(x)=-\frac{A V_\xi P_{t+1}}{c+V_\xi P_{t+1}}x,\quad
 P_t=1+\frac{A^2c V_\xi P_{t+1}}{c+V_\xi P_{t+1}},\quad
 d_t=d_{t+1}+P_{t+1}V_\epsilon.}
$$
This feedback is optimal among all controls measurable from the specified past, not merely linear controls. It depends on $V_\xi$ and **does not depend on $V_\epsilon$**, though the minimal cost does. There is no cost-relevant $u_T$ in the displayed objective.

The backward <Riccati recurrence> iterates $F(P)=1+A^2cV_\xi P/(c+V_\xi P)$ from $1$. It is increasing, maps into $[1,1+A^2c]$, and has exactly one positive <fixed point>. Consequently it converges to
$$
\boxed{P=\frac{-b+\sqrt{b^2+4cV_\xi}}{2V_\xi},\quad
 b=c-V_\xi-A^2cV_\xi,\qquad
 u^*(x)=-\frac{A V_\xi P}{c+V_\xi P}x.}
$$
As $T\to\infty$, the finite-horizon feedback at any fixed time tends to this feedback. The closed-loop multiplier is $A c/(c+V_\xi P)$, and its squared-mean factor $\alpha=V_\xi A^2c^2/(c+V_\xi P)^2$ is less than one, since $(P-1)/P=A^2cV_\xi/(c+V_\xi P)$ and $\alpha=(P-1)c/[P(c+V_\xi P)]$. Thus the second moment tends to $V_\epsilon/(1-\alpha)$. The finite-horizon value has $d_1=V_\epsilon\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}P_{t+1}$; its average converges by Cesaro summation. For a fixed initial state of finite second moment,
$$
\boxed{\lim_{T\to\infty}\frac1T\inf\mathbb E[\text{total cost}]=P V_\epsilon.}
$$
The same average is attained by the stationary feedback, by the stationary <Bellman equation> and bounded second moments.

= 29E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Asymptotic Methods}

= Solution
{parent=29E}

The exponent $\Phi(t)=\tfrac i2(t-t^{-1})$ has saddles $t=\pm i$, with $\Phi(-i)=1$ and $\Phi(i)=-1$. Since the integrand is analytic off the origin, deform the contour to the unit <circle> and rotate $t=-i e^{i\theta}$. This gives the exact representation
$$
 I_0(x)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}e^{x\cos\theta}\,d\theta.
$$
The dominant saddle at $\theta=0$ is a steepest-descent direction for the <method of steepest descent>: $\cos\theta=1-\theta^2/2+\theta^4/24-\cdots$. Set $\theta=s/\sqrt x$ and expand the analytic amplitude against $e^{-s^2/2}$. Integrating its Gaussian moments gives the leading factor $e^x/\sqrt{2\pi x}$; the rest of the contour is exponentially smaller than this dominant contribution outside any fixed neighbourhood of the maximum.

To obtain every coefficient efficiently, the <integral> satisfies $x^2I_0''+xI_0'-x^2I_0=0$, by differentiating and integrating the <derivative> of $\sin\theta\,e^{x\cos\theta}$. Substitute $I_0=e^x x^{-1/2}\sum a_nx^{-n}/\sqrt{2\pi}$ to get $a_0=1$ and $a_n=(2n-1)^2a_{n-1}/(8n)$. Therefore the full <asymptotic expansion> is
$$
\boxed{I_0(x)\sim\frac{e^x}{\sqrt{2\pi x}}
 \sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{((2n-1)!!)^2}{n!\,8^n x^n}
 =\frac{e^x}{\sqrt{2\pi x}}\left(1+\frac1{8x}+\frac9{128x^2}+\frac{225}{3072x^3}+\cdots\right).}
$$
Here $(-1)!!=1$. For each fixed truncation at $n=N$, the relative remainder is $O(x^{-N-1})$ as $x\to+\infty$. The infinite <series> is generally divergent, and this all-orders expansion does not purport to resolve exponentially small saddle contributions or their <Stokes phenomenon>.

= 30A
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= Solution
{parent=30A}

The <centre manifold theorem> gives, near an equilibrium of a sufficiently smooth system, a local invariant <manifold> tangent to the <generalized eigenspaces> with zero-real-part <eigenvalues>, expressible as a <graph of a function> over them. Its reduced dynamics govern local equilibria and their stability in the presence of strictly stable transverse directions. The <manifold> need not be unique or analytic; finite Taylor jets can nevertheless be determined from invariance. Treating a parameter as a variable with $\dot\mu=0$ gives the <extended centre manifold for a parameter>.

At $r=1$ the linearized $(x,y)$ block has <eigenvalues> $0,-2$, and the $z$ direction has <eigenvalue> $-1$. Thus the origin is a <nonhyperbolic equilibrium>, with **stable <dimension> two and non-extended centre <dimension> one**. The extended centre <dimension> is two. The transformed equations are
$$
\begin{aligned}
 \dot u&=\tfrac12\mu(u+v)+\tfrac a2(u+v)^3-\tfrac12(u-v)z,\\
 \dot v&=-2v-\tfrac12\mu(u+v)+\tfrac a2(u+v)^3+\tfrac12(u-v)z,\\
 \dot z&=u^2-v^2-z,\qquad\dot\mu=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <graph of a function> satisfies $V(0,0)=Z(0,0)=0$ and $DV(0,0)=DZ(0,0)=0$, with invariance equations $V_u\dot u=\dot v$ and $Z_u\dot u=\dot z$. Symmetry permits $V$ odd and $Z$ even in $u$. Giving $u$ weight one and $\mu$ weight two, comparison yields
$$
 Z=u^2+O_{\rm w}(4),\qquad
 V=-\frac14\mu u+\frac{a+1}{4}u^3+O_{\rm w}(5),\qquad
 \boxed{\dot u=\frac12\mu u+\frac{a-1}{2}u^3+O_{\rm w}(5).}
$$
Here $O_{\rm w}(j)$ means terms of weighted order at least $j$ under the stated scaling. If $a<1$, the <pitchfork bifurcation> is supercritical: stable branches $u\sim\pm\sqrt{\mu/(1-a)}$ appear for $\mu>0$ as the origin loses stability. If $a>1$, it is a <subcritical pitchfork bifurcation>: unstable branches occur for $\mu<0$, while the origin is stable there.

For $a=1$, assign $\mu$ weight four. The leading cubic terms in $\dot u$ cancel. The invariance equations now give $V=u^3/2+O_{\rm w}(5)$ and $Z=u^2+0\,u^4+O_{\rm w}(6)$: $Z_u\dot u$ first contributes at weight six and $v^2$ also first contributes there. Substitution then gives
$$
\boxed{\dot u=\frac12\mu u+u^5+O_{\rm w}(7),\qquad
 u\sim\pm(-\mu/2)^{1/4}\quad(\mu<0).}
$$
The <derivative> along the nonzero branches is $-2\mu>0$, so they are unstable. The origin is stable for $\mu<0$, unstable for $\mu>0$, and nonlinearly unstable at $\mu=0$ because the leading term is $+u^5$. This is a degenerate subcritical <pitchfork bifurcation>.

